// pageio.nv — the same walk, written as a loop instead of a protocol.
//
// `btree.step` hands the host a `PageRequest` and waits.  That is the
// shape that has no effects at all, and it is the shape a host with an
// event loop, a batching reader or an async pager wants.  A host with
// a plain file wants a function call.  This module is that: a trait
// with one effect parameter, and drivers that bind it.
//
//     trait PageIo[e]                  the host's four operations
//     pub fn run<S: PageIo[e]>(…) [e]  charged what the impl costs
//
// SPEC § 5.6 is what makes this legal in a `core` package.  A free
// function cannot be `[e]`, but a function that BINDS the effect
// parameter of one of its own trait bounds can, and the row means
// "whatever the impl behind `S` supplies".  `pager-nv` supplies
// `[fs]`, so a program that calls `run` with a pager is charged
// `[fs]`; a test that supplies an in-memory page table implements
// `PageIo[]` and is charged nothing.  Neither charge lands on this
// package, whose budget stays `[]`.
//
// WHICH SHAPE TO USE
//
// `btree.step` is the primitive and this is built on it — a host that
// needs to interleave, batch or suspend must use `step`, because a
// call into `run` does not come back until the walk is over.  Use
// `run` when the pager is synchronous, which is what pager-nv is.

use cell
use btree

// The four things a host can do with a page, and what each costs.
//
// The trait binds one effect parameter, `e`, and every member uses it
// (SPEC § 5.6 requires both).  An impl supplies the set its own
// implementation actually costs: `impl PageIo[fs] for Pager` in
// pager-nv, `impl PageIo[] for PageTable` in a test.
pub trait PageIo[e]
    // The whole page stored under this id, header included.
    fn fetch(self, page_id: Int) -> Result<[Int], IoFault> [e]
    // Store these bytes under this id.
    fn store(self, page_id: Int, bytes: [Int]) -> Result<Unit, IoFault> [e]
    // Allocate a page of this kind and answer with its id.
    fn allocate(self, kind: Int) -> Result<Int, IoFault> [e]
    // Release a page that is no longer reachable.
    fn release(self, page_id: Int) -> Result<Unit, IoFault> [e]

// Everything a finished walk produced.
//
// `rows` is empty for an insert or a delete, and `tree` is the tree to
// keep — an insert that split the root moved it.
pub struct Outcome
    rows: [[cell.Cell]]
    keys: [Int]
    rows_affected: Int
    tree: btree.Tree

// Run a cursor to completion, answering every request it makes.
//
// The row is `[e]`: this call costs exactly what `S`'s own `PageIo`
// impl costs, and that impl is declared where the host is.  A `core`
// package can therefore take a host's page store without leaving its
// own budget, which is the whole reason SPEC § 5.6 gives functions the
// binding.
//
// ```novo
// use btree
// use pageio
//
// fn all_rows<S: pageio.PageIo[e]>(io: S, t: btree.Tree) -> Int [e]
//     match pageio.run(io, btree.scan(t))
//         Ok(out) => out.rows_affected
//         Err(f)  => 0
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("a core walk, charged what the host's impl costs")
// ```
pub fn run<S: PageIo[e]>(io: S, start: btree.Cursor) -> Result<Outcome, IoFault> [e]
    todo("btree-nv.pageio.run")

// Run a cursor until its next row, so a caller can stop early.
//
// Answers `None` when the walk finished without another row.  A
// `LIMIT` that stops after ten rows of a million-row table is the
// reason this exists beside `run`.
pub fn next_row<S: PageIo[e]>(io: S, c: btree.Cursor) -> Result<?NextRow, IoFault> [e]
    todo("btree-nv.pageio.next_row")

// One row, its key, and the cursor to continue from.
pub struct NextRow
    key: Int
    row: [cell.Cell]
    cursor: btree.Cursor

// Why the host could not answer a request.
//
// A `BtreeError` is the tree saying the bytes were wrong; an
// `IoFault` is the host saying it could not get them.  They are two
// types because the two have different readers: one is a bug in
// whatever wrote the file, the other is a disk.
pub enum IoFault
    // The host could not read this page.
    ReadFailed(page_id: Int, why: Str)
    // The host could not store this page.
    WriteFailed(page_id: Int, why: Str)
    // The host had no page to give — the store is full, or read-only.
    AllocFailed(why: Str)
    // The tree stopped on its own; the host performed everything asked
    // of it.
    Walk(cause: btree.BtreeError)

// The human sentence behind each way a host can fail a request.
impl Error for IoFault
    fn message(self) -> Str
        todo("btree-nv.pageio.IoFault.message")
